, q2 254. (Table 4 DN900 class 2: 80, 128. Different because battery powered class 2 has ratio 125... wait ratio Q3/Q1=63 means Q1 = 10000/63 = 158.73. Yes.)\n\nTable 6 — Indicating device, Reduced bore, AC mains: columns meter_size, indicating_range_max (m³), indicating_range_min (m³), verification_scale_interval_max (m³).\nRows:\n- DN40, DN50, DN65: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- DN80: 9999999, 999999, 0.0001\n- DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN250: 9999999, 9999999, 0.001\n- DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n\nTable 7 — Reduced bore, Battery:\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- DN65: 9999999, 99999, 0.001\n- DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN200: 9999999, 999999, 0.01\n- DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- DN500, DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\n\nTable 8 — Full bore, AC mains:\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- DN65: 9999999, 999999, 0.0001\n- DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN200, DN250, DN300: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\n- DN700–DN1200 (DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200): 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\n- DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650, DN1800: 999999999, 99999999, 1\n\nTable 9 — Full bore, Battery:\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.001\n- DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.01\n- DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- DN600: 99999999, 9999999, 0.1\n- DN700, DN750, DN800: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\n- DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650: 999999999, 99999999, 1\n\nFor indicating device tables, the meter_size column groups multiple sizes. In rows I'll keep meter_sizes as a string list like \"DN40, DN50, DN65\" or split into individual rows? The schema rows are objects per row. I'll keep the grouped sizes as given: \"meter_sizes\": \"DN40, DN50, DN65\". Or split each size into its own row for machine-readability. Splitting is cleaner for extraction. But then \"rows\" explode — that's fine, it's accurate. Hmm, splitting is arguably better since each size gets its values. I'll split per size: e.g., row {meter_size: \"DN40\", indicating_range_max: 9999999, ...}. Wait but is the range max/min \"maximum value\"/\"minimum value\" of indicating range — yes columns: Indicating range (maximum value), Indicating range (minimum value), Verification scale interval (maximum value).\n\nActually, note the column order is odd: \"Indicating range (maximum value)\" then \"(minimum value)\" — I'll transcribe as-is.\n\nI'll split grouped sizes into individual rows for Tables 6–9. That gives ~50 rows each? Table 8: DN40..DN1800 = 29 sizes. Table 9: 26 sizes. Fine.\n\nActually, to keep the output manageable, maybe keep grouped as-is (rows as printed). The instructions say preserve tables. Grouped rows are faithful to source. But splitting makes each row uniform. I'll split — more useful downstream. Hmm, it increases output length significantly. Total rows: T2:16, T3:26, T4:45, T5:38, T6:22, T7:20, T8:29, T9:26 → ~222 rows. That's a lot of JSON but acceptable.\n\nAlternatively keep grouped rows as printed for indicating tables (9 rows each). I'll split anyway for consistency; each row = one meter size.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider: for matrix_tables, the schema example keeps one row per source row (G2.5, G1.6). Splitting grouped cells is a transformation but each resulting row is true to the data. I'll split.\n\nmodel_level characteristics: Should I populate? The R137 example populates model_level from the matrix table. Here, with 4 config-dependent tables, model_level would need axis-aware modeling. I'll use config_level instead for the flow attributes? config_level values have {condition, value}. With ~220 combos, config_level entries would be as big as the tables. Since matrix_tables fully preserve the data, I'll populate a modest model_level for attributes that are uniform per model across configs... Actually almost everything varies.\n\nDecision: I'll populate config_level with the key class/range attributes (pressure loss, flow profile sensitivity, power supply options) and rely on matrix_tables for the full flow-rate/indicating matrices. I'll add model_level entries for a few per-size attributes? Hmm, maybe skip model_level to avoid inconsistency, or include one: e.g., \"meter_size\" not meaningful.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe populate model_level for q3 (permanent flow rate) per model where model = \"DN size\"? But q3 differs by power and class for the same DN (e.g., DN40: AC class1 Q3=25; battery class1 Q3=25 — same; DN40 full bore: 40). So q3 for DN40 ∈ {25 (reduced), 40 (full)}. Not single-valued per size. So model_level per DN doesn't work cleanly. Confirmed: matrix_tables is the right vehicle; config_level for axis-level attributes.\n\nI'll define config_level entries:\n1. flow_profile_sensitivity_class — axis: meter_size\n   - {condition: \"smaller than DN250 (0 × DN upstream, 0 × DN downstream)\", value: \"U0 and D0\"}\n   - {condition: \"DN250 and larger (3 × DN upstream, 0 × DN downstream)\", value: \"U3 and D0\"}\n2. pressure_loss_class — axis: bore_type_and_meter_size\n   - {condition: \"reduced bore, DN40 and DN50\", value: \"Δp 40\"}\n   - {condition: \"reduced bore, larger than DN50\", value: \"Δp 63\"}\n   - {condition: \"(virtual) full bore, DN40 and DN50\", value: \"Δp 10\"}\n   - {condition: \"(virtual) full bore, DN65 to DN200\", value: \"Δp 16\"}\n   - {condition: \"(virtual) full bore, DN250 and larger\", value: \"Δp 10\"}\n3. maximum_pressure_loss — same axis, values 0.40/0.63/0.10/0.16/0.10 bar.\n4. available_flow_rate_tables? No.\n\nPower supply: type_level with separate entries as computed.\n\nNow the certificate fields:\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: \"3528374\"\n- page_total: 12\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: \"2022-08-30\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\nAlso revision is in the number (\"revision 7\"). Schema has revision_history; certificate-level revision? Not in schema. The number field: keep as given \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\". Maybe add \"revision\": not in schema. Leave; revision_history captures current revision 7.\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\" — I'll use name \"NMi Certin B.V.\" and oiml_issuer_id \"NL1\".\n- address_lines: [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"]\n- person_responsible: \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\"\n- person_title: not given (Certification Board? The signature block says \"Certification Board\"). Hmm, \"Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\" and near signature \"Certification Board\". Not clearly a title. I'll omit person_title.\n- phone: \"+31 88 636 2332\"\n- email: \"certin@nmi.nl\"\n- website: \"www.nmi.nl\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\napplicants: [{name: \"ABB Limited\", address_lines: [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"]}]\n\nmanufacturers: [{ABB Limited, same address}, plus production locations? I decided to include. Let me include all four production locations as manufacturers with their addresses:\n- ABB Limited, Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom\n- ABB Engineering (Shanghai) Ltd., No. 4528, Kangxin Highway, Pudong New District, Shanghai, 201319, P.R. China\n- ABB Inc., 125 E. County Line Road, Warminster, PA 18974, United States of America (USA)\n- ABB India Limited, Plot No. 5 & 6, 2nd Phase, Peenya Industrial Area, Bengaluru – 560058, Karnataka India\n\nHmm — is it safe to call them \"manufacturers\"? \"The water meter is produced at one of the following production locations\". Producing = manufacturing. Yes, include.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4\"] — hmm. The original: \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\". So maybe designations: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4XY.Z\"? No — FET4 has no XY.Z per text; the asterisk applies. I'll list: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4\"]. Include the asterisk? No, footnote-style markers shouldn't be in labels. The description will explain.\n- module_designation: null (or \"Not applicable\"). I'll use null. Actually schema: \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. Water meters — I'll put null.\n- description: \"Electromagnetic water meter AquaMaster4. FEW4XY.Z flow sensor types: X = 1 or 3 (standard or advanced), Y = 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only), Z = R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore); FET4 transmitter variants.\"\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"AquaMaster4\"\n- models: 29 entries with attributes.\n\nLet me construct models:\n\nSizes and availability (from tables 2–5):\n1. DN40: reduced AC (T2), reduced battery (T3), full AC (T4), full battery (T5) → attributes: {meter_size_mm: 40, reduced_bore: true, full_bore: true, ac_mains_powered: true, battery_powered: true}\n2. DN50: same as DN40.\n3. DN65: same (T3 has DN65 battery; T2 AC; T4/T5 full) → all true.\n4. DN80: all true.\n5. DN100: all true.\n6. DN125: all true.\n7. DN150: all true.\n8. DN200: all true.\n9. DN250: all true.\n10. DN300: all true.\n11. DN350: reduced AC: no; reduced battery: yes; full AC: yes; full battery: yes.\n12. DN400: same as 350.\n13. DN450: same.\n14. DN500: same.\n15. DN600: same.\n16. DN700: reduced: no; full AC: yes; full battery: yes.\n17. DN750: same as 700.\n18. DN800: same.\n19. DN900: full AC (classes 1&2), full battery (class 2 only).\n20. DN1000: full AC both, full battery class 2 only.\n21. DN1050: same.\n22. DN1100: same.\n23. DN1200: same.\n24. DN1350: full AC both classes, full battery class 2 only.\n25. DN1400: same.\n26. DN1500: same.\n27. DN1600: same.\n28. DN1650: same.\n29. DN1800: full AC only (classes 1&2), no battery.\n\nI'll encode attributes as booleans: reduced_bore_ac_mains, reduced_bore_battery, full_bore_ac_mains, full_bore_battery. Plus accuracy classes? Keep it simple:\n\nattributes: {\"reduced_bore_ac_mains\": true/false, \"reduced_bore_battery_renewable\": bool, \"full_bore_ac_mains\": bool, \"full_bore_battery_renewable\": bool}\n\nThat's 29 × 4 booleans. Fine.\n\nComponents: The cert doesn't explicitly name sub-assemblies as separate certified components (flow sensor FEW4 and transmitter FET4 are part of the type designation). Should I add components? \"An electromagnetic water meter Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*\" — FEW4 = flow sensor, FET4 = electronic transmitter/calculator. The cert type covers both. I could add components:\n- role: \"flow sensor\", type_designations: [\"FEW4XY.Z\"]\n- role: \"electronic transmitter / indicating device\", type_designations: [\"FET4\"]\n\nThis is reasonable interpretation — FEW4 is the sensor, FET4 the transmitter. Actually ABB AquaMaster: FEW = flow electromagnet water? FET = flow transmitter. The revision history mentions \"for the flow transmitter\" (revision 3: \"E2 class and O class for the flow transmitter\"), confirming FET4 is the flow transmitter. And \"for both flow sensor and flow transmitter\" in Table 1 temperature range — confirms two sub-assemblies. I'll include components:\n- {role: \"flow sensor\", type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\"], characteristics: {}}\n- {role: \"flow transmitter\", type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FET4\"]}\n\nAlso bore variants: Z = R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore), F (full bore) — mention in description.\n\nFootnotes: The asterisk \"* With X being 1 or 3...\" — that's a footnote definition. marker \"*\", text \"With X being 1 or 3 (standard or advanced), Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only) and Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\" Add to footnotes[]. Then type_designations could carry footnote_markers? Values have footnote_markers; type_designations are strings. I'll add the footnote and reference in description.\n\nmatrix_tables: 8 entries. Names: \n- \"Table 2 General characteristics of the family of instruments — Reduced bore - AC mains powered\"\n- etc. Keep name concise: \"reduced_bore_ac_mains_flow_rates\" plus description. I'll use the printed names.\n\nColumns: [\"meter_size\", \"accuracy_class\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q3_q1_ratio\"], column_units: [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null].\n\naccuracy_class value: strings \"1\", \"2\", \"1 & 2\". Keep as string \"1 & 2\"? Or numeric where single. Mixed types OK. I'll use strings: \"1\", \"2\", \"1 & 2\".\n\nFor indicating tables columns: [\"meter_size\", \"indicating_range_max\", \"indicating_range_min\", \"verification_scale_interval_max\"], units [null, \"m³\", \"m³\", \"m³\"].\n\nWait — should I split grouped meter sizes in indicating tables? Let me split for consistency: each row one meter_size.\n\nTable 6 split rows (reduced, AC):\nDN40: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\nDN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\nDN65: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\nDN80: 9999999, 999999, 0.0001\nDN100: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\nDN125: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\nDN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\nDN200: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\nDN250: 9999999, 9999999, 0.001\nDN300: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\nDN350: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\nDN400: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\nDN450: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\nDN500: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\nDN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n\nTable 7 (reduced, battery):\nDN40: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\nDN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\nDN65: 9999999, 99999, 0.001\nDN80: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\nDN100: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\nDN125: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\nDN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\nDN200: 9999999, 999999, 0.01\nDN250: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\nDN300: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\nDN350: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\nDN400: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\nDN450: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\nDN500: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\nDN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\n\nTable 8 (full, AC):\nDN40: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\nDN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\nDN65: 9999999, 999999, 0.0001\nDN80: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\nDN100: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\nDN125: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\nDN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\nDN200: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\nDN250: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\nDN300: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\nDN350: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\nDN400: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\nDN450: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\nDN500: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\nDN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\nDN700: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\nDN750: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\nDN800: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\nDN900: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\nDN1000: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\nDN1050: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\nDN1100: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\nDN1200: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\nDN1350: 999999999, 99999999, 1\nDN1400: 999999999, 99999999, 1\nDN1500: 999999999, 99999999, 1\nDN1600: 999999999, 99999999, 1\nDN1650: 999999999, 99999999, 1\nDN1800: 999999999, 99999999, 1\n\nTable 9 (full, battery):\nDN40: 9999999, 99999, 0.001\nDN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.001\nDN65: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\nDN80: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\nDN100: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\nDN125: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\nDN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.01\nDN200: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\nDN250: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\nDN300: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\nDN350: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\nDN400: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\nDN450: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\nDN500: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\nDN600: 99999999, 9999999, 0.1\nDN700: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\nDN750: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\nDN800: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\nDN900: 999999999, 99999999, 1\nDN1000: 999999999, 99999999, 1\nDN1050: 999999999, 99999999, 1\nDN1100: 999999999, 99999999, 1\nDN1200: 999999999, 99999999, 1\nDN1350: 999999999, 99999999, 1\nDN1400: 999999999, 99999999, 1\nDN1500: 999999999, 99999999, 1\nDN1600: 999999999, 99999999, 1\nDN1650: 999999999, 99999999, 1\n\nOK. Now the full JSON. Also \"documentation\" — add test_reports entry {id: \"T11851-6\", role: \"documentation_file\"}. Label says \"Documentation folder no. T11851-6\".\n\nRevision history revisions as strings: \"initial\", \"1\", ..., \"7\".\n\nRecommendation:\n{\"id\": \"R49-1\", \"edition\": 2013, \"scheme\": \"A\", \"accuracy_classes\": [1, 2]}\n\nHmm, amendment: null/omit. Omit.\n\nNow assemble characteristics:\n\ntype_level:\n- measuring_principle: {value: \"Electromagnetic\"}\n- environmental_class: {value: \"O\"} \n- environmental_class_installation: {value: \"installed outdoors\"}? I'll fold into one string: environmental_class: {value: \"O (installed outdoors)\"}. Hmm — canonical labels should be clean; value can be string. I'll do two entries: environmental_class: {value: \"O\"}; environment_installation: {value: \"installed outdoors\"}. Actually simpler: keep \"O (installed outdoors)\" as the value string. Decide: environmental_class: {value: \"O\"} and add environmental_class_note... no. Final: environmental_class value \"O\", and environment value \"installed outdoors\" as separate characteristic \"installation_environment\". Hmm, that label isn't from source. I'll just use the combined string value \"O (installed outdoors)\". It's fine.\n\n- electromagnetic_environment: {value: \"E2\"}\n- mechanical_environment_class: {value: \"M1\"} — source \"Mechanical class M1\". Label: mechanical_class.\n- temperature_range_ambient: {min:-25, max:55}, \"°C\"\n- water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"}\n- water_temperature_range: {min:0.1, max:50}, \"°C\"\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit: \"MPa\"} — plus secondary mention 16 bar; I'll add second entry? Can't duplicate label. Add \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" only with MPa. Fine. Or include note string? Keep MPa.\n- orientation: {value: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\"} → value string \"all positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\". Keep as given: \"All positions (Horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\". I'll normalize slightly: \"all positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\". Keep close to source.\n- reverse_flow: {value: \"intended to measure reverse flow\"} — or \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\". Keep source-ish: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\". Hmm value long but OK.\n- power_supply_voltage_ac_mains: {min:85, max:240}, \"V\"\n- power_supply_frequency: {min:50, max:60}, \"Hz\"\n- power_supply_voltage_dc: {min:6, max:32}, \"V\" (renewable energy / DC powered)\n- battery_quantity: {value: 2}\n- battery_cell_voltage: {value: 3.6, \"V\"}\n- battery_operating_voltage_range: {min:2.9, max:3.6}, \"V\"\n- software_identification: big string (as discussed)\n- automatic_checking_facility: {value: \"Type P (permanent)\"}\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class → config_level\n- pressure_loss_class → config_level\n\nLabels check: no LaTeX, no footnote markers. Good.\n\nconfig_level entries as planned.\n\nDo I need model_level at all? The R137 example implies populating both. Here, I'll skip model_level (justify: variation captured via matrix_tables and config axes). Actually, hmm, the grader may check for model_level when a family exists. But forcing 200-row model_level would be redundant with matrix_tables. The R60 example: model_family.models with model_id \"Emax=25t\" etc. and model_level for maximum_capacity. For our case, models are DN sizes; a model_level attribute that's single-valued per model... e.g., nothing really. I'll include model_family without model_level entries; matrix_tables carry the data. I think that's the best fidelity trade-off.\n\nWait — maybe include one model_level: \"nominal_size\"? Trivial. Skip.\n\nComponents with sub-characteristics: none needed beyond type designations.\n\nNow, test report dates ISO:\n- NMi-2254799-01: 2020-08-14, 235 pages\n- NMi-2254799-03: 2020-09-30, 44\n- NMi-2254799-04: 2020-12-24, 180\n- NMi-2564429-01: 2021-08-02, 26\n- NMi-2496280-01: 2021-08-26, 29\n- NMi-2496280-02: 2021-11-23, 25\n- T11851-6 documentation_file, no date/pages → omit absent fields.\n\nRoles: first six = \"type_evaluation_report\". The text: \"conformity was established by the results of tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\". These are the OIML Type Evaluation Report(s). I'll use \"type_evaluation_report\" for the six.\n\nRevision history dates:\n- initial: 2020-08-14 \"First issue.\"\n- 1: 2020-09-30 \"Additional evaluation of reduced bore larger sizes up to DN600.\"\n- 2: 2020-12-24 \"Additional evaluation of (virtual) full bore sensor and additional software version.\"\n- 3: 2021-08-02 \"Additional evaluation for extending temperature to –25 °C and for E2 class and O class for the flow transmitter. Addition of software version.\"\n- 4: 2021-08-26 \"Addition of full-bore sizes up to DN1200.\"\n- 5: 2021-11-23 \"Addition of full-bore sizes up to DN1800.\"\n- 6: 2022-05-20 \"Addition of accuracy class 1 to DN1800.\"\n- 7: 2022-08-30 \"Software version updates due to addition of microprocessors.\"\n\nFootnotes: marker \"*\" with the X/Y/Z text.\n\nAlso note: certificate.number should maybe include revision? Schema example shows number plain. Keep \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\". Could add \"revision\": \"7\"? Not in certificate schema. Skip.\n\ncertificate.date_issued: \"2022-08-30\" (date at bottom \"30 August 2022\").\n\nNow write JSON. Careful with escaping — no special issues. Use ³ character directly (fine in JSON).\n\nLet me now double check a few table values:\n\nTable 2 DN200: 0,63 / 1,008 / 630 / 787,5 / 1000 ✓.\nTable 3 DN65 class1: 0,39375 / 0,63 / 63 / 78,75 / 160 ✓.\nTable 5 DN600 class1: 78,75 / 126 / 6300 / 7875 / 80 ✓; class2: 39,375 / 63 / ... / 160 ✓.\nTable 4 DN1100 class1: 253,9 / 406,2 ✓ (note class2 128/204,8).\nTable 4 DN900 class1: 158,7 / 253,9 ✓.\nTable 5 DN1100: class 2: 254 / 406,3 ✓.\n\nPower supply: \"AC mains: 85 –240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz\" → 85–240 V. \"Renewable energy power (DC powered): 6 – 32 V\". \"Replaceable Battery: 2x 3,6 V (operating range: 2,9 - 3,6 V)\".\n\nNow matrix table descriptions: include the note about Q1/Q2/Q3/Q4 freely chosen? I'll add to description of each flow table or a shared note. Add \"footnotes\"? The notes aren't numbered footnotes. I'll include the note in description for each flow table: \"Flow rates Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4 may be freely chosen provided Q3 and Q3/Q1 from R49-1 §4.1; Q1, Q2 are minimum values with Q2/Q1 = 1.6; Q3, Q4 are maximum values with Q4/Q3 = 1.25; Q3/Q1 at least 40.\"\n\nKeep description shorter: \"Q1 and Q2 are minimum values (Q2/Q1 = 1.6); Q3 and Q4 are maximum values (Q4/Q3 = 1.25); Q3/Q1 ≥ 40, selected per OIML R49-1:2013 §4.1.\"\n\nAvoid \"≥\"? It's fine in JSON strings. Rule prohibits LaTeX and footnote markers; ≥ okay but I'll write \"at least 40\".\n\nNow assemble the models list. model_id: \"DN40\" etc. label maybe \"Meter size DN40\". attributes: four booleans.\n\nDN40–DN300 (sizes 1–10): all four true.\nDN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600: reduced_bore_ac_mains: false, reduced_bore_battery_renewable: true, full_bore_ac_mains: true, full_bore_battery_renewable: true.\nDN700–DN800: reduced false, false; full true, true.\nDN900–DN1650: full AC true; full battery true (but class restrictions — ignore, tables capture it).\nDN1800: full AC true; full battery false.\n\nNames of attributes: \"reduced_bore_ac_mains_powered\", \"reduced_bore_battery_renewable_powered\", \"full_bore_ac_mains_powered\", \"full_bore_battery_renewable_powered\". Slightly long but clear. Or \"availability\": list of config keys. Booleans fine.\n\nAlso each model could carry nominal_size_mm numeric. Add attribute \"meter_size_mm\": 40 etc. Good—gives each model a numeric attribute.\n\ncomponents: \n1. {role: \"flow sensor\", type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\"], characteristics: {bore_variants?}} — maybe characteristics: {\"bore_variants\": {\"value\": \"R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore), F (full bore)\"}}? The X/Y/Z legend applies to FEW4XY.Z. I'll add a characteristics object: {\"version_code_x\": {\"value\": \"1 or 3 (standard or advanced)\"}, \"version_code_y\": {\"value\": \"1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only)\"}, \"bore_code_z\": {\"value\": \"R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore), F (full bore)\"}}. Values are strings — acceptable.\n2. {role: \"flow transmitter (indicating device)\", type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FET4\"]}\n\nRole naming: \"flow_sensor\" or \"Flow sensor\"? Schema shows role free-text. I'll use \"flow sensor\" / \"flow transmitter\".\n\nHmm, is FET4 the indicating device? AquaMaster4: FEW4 sensor + FET4 transmitter with integral display. Tables 6–9 are \"indicating device\" characteristics. I'll say role \"flow transmitter / indicating device\".\n\nNow the certificate-level \"scheme\": \"A\".\n\nEverything assembled. Length is large but OK.\n\nOne more check: \"Number R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 7\" — number field: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\". Good.\n\nPage total: 12.\n\nOIML issuer id: NL1 (from \"OIML Issuing Authority NL1\" and certificate number contains NL1).\n\nDate issued: 30 August 2022 → \"2022-08-30\".\n\nWrite final JSON without fences.\n\nFor value objects in type_level, include unit_symbol null when none? Schema shows {value, unit_symbol/unit_id, footnote_markers}. I'll include unit_symbol only when applicable, and footnote_markers only when present. The example shape includes both keys; I'll include unit_symbol: null where no unit for consistency, but omit footnote_markers when empty to reduce noise. Actually rule 2 shape shows unit_symbol and unit_id always. To be safe include unit_symbol (null when none) and unit_id: null? Including unit_id: null everywhere adds bulk; the rules say \"Leave unit_id: null unless certain\". I'll include unit_id: null in entries where unit_symbol present, and for entries without units, just {value, unit_symbol: null}. Hmm consistency... I'll include \"unit_symbol\" always and \"unit_id\" always null (we're not certain of unitsdb IDs). Actually for m³/h, unitsdb id unknown — null. °C → u:degree_Celsius is given in the example as acceptable; I could use it for °C. The example says unit_id \"u:degree_Celsius\" | null and \"Leave unit_id: null unless you are certain\". I'm fairly confident u:degree_Celsius exists per example. I'll set unit_id \"u:degree_Celsius\" for °C, and null elsewhere. For m³/h, m³, MPa, bar, V, Hz — leave null. Hmm, V could be u:volt, Hz u:hertz — likely exist but I'm not certain of exact IDs; keep null per instruction to be conservative. Actually the instruction example lists \"V\" and \"Hz\" as unit_symbol examples, and unit_id \"u:degree_Celsius\" example. I'll only fill u:degree_Celsius for temperature.\n\nLet me now also double-check Table 3 DN500: class1 ratio 40, class2 ratio 100 — yes as transcribed. DN600 class1 40, class2 100 ✓.\n\nTable 5 DN900 row: \"DN900, 2, 158,7, 254, 10000, 12500, 63\" → q1 158.7, q2 254 ✓.\n\nTable 5 DN1000: 160, 250 (not 256!) — \"DN1000 2 160 250 16000 20000 100\". Yes 250. And DN1050 same. DN1100/1200: 254, 406.3. OK.\n\nTable 4 DN1100/1200 class 2: 128, 204.8 ✓.\n\nVerification scale interval values: 0,0001 → 0.0001; 0,01 → 0.01; 0,1 → 0.1; 1 → 1. ✓\n\nNow, the \"Please note\" bullets: I'll include in description fields of the four flow tables.\n\nAlso Table 2 note appears after Table 3 (page 4) too — applies to tables 2/3. Tables 4/5 note appears after table 5 (page 5) and after table 7's table on page 7 — wait, page 7 note is after the continuation of Table 4 (full bore AC). Actually structure: Table 4 spans pages 6–7, note after page 7 portion. Table 5 spans pages 8–9 with note after page 9. So each of tables 2,3 (note on page 4 covers both) and 4,5 get the note. I'll add the same description note to all four flow tables.\n\nFinal check on the \"checked facilities\": \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\" → automatic_checking_facility: {value: \"Type P (permanent)\"} plus maybe facility_type. I'll do: checking_facility_type: {value: \"P (permanent) automatic\"}. Hmm keep source: checking_facilities: {value: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"}. Label canonical: automatic_checking_facility. Fine.\n\nSoftware: I'll write:\nsoftware_identification: {value: \"Software part code 3KXF208402U0113: version 03.00.03 (checksum B26AFE6B); version 03.00.04 (checksum 6E98963A); version 03.02.00 (checksum E12E5C04); version 03.03.02 (checksum D4D1E40B); version 03.03.03 (checksum CEEF45F1). Software part code 3KXF208402U0313: version 02.00.02 (checksum 84EBD1C8). Software part code 3KXF208402U0513: version 02.00.01 (checksum C28EAA16).\"}\n\nOK. Compose final JSON now.\n\nAlso, should certificate include \"revision\"? Not in schema — skip.\n\nTest reports: also the OIML Type Evaluation Report mention. Fine.\n\nWrite it.\n\nOne consideration: matrix_tables column names: use \"q1\",\"q2\",\"q3\",\"q4\",\"ratio_q3_q1\". Rows keys match column names. accuracy_class values: \"1\", \"2\", \"1 & 2\".\n\nmeter_size values: \"DN40\" strings.\n\nRow counts final:\nT2: 16 rows. T3: 26. T4: 45. T5: 38. T6: 15. T7: 15. T8: 29. T9: 28.\n\nLet me verify T9 count: DN40,50,65,80,100,125,150,200,250,300,350,400,450,500,600,700,750,800,900,1000,1050,1100,1200,1350,1400,1500,1600,1650 = 28 ✓.\nT8: same plus DN1800 = 29 ✓.\nT6: DN40–DN600 = 15 ✓. T7: 15 ✓.\n\nAlright, writing the JSON. Must be careful and complete. I'll write compactly but valid.",
